target/arm: read the generic timer counters without the BQL

CNTPCT/CNTVCT (and the FEAT_ECV SS views) are ARM_CP_IO, so every read
takes the BQL in get_cp_reg64 and ends the TB. iOS userland reads
CNTVCT_EL0 for every mach_absolute_time(); with 8 vCPUs and a display
refresh holding the BQL in the main loop, the vCPUs spend about half of
their time waiting for it.

The readfns only touch per-CPU state and QEMU_CLOCK_VIRTUAL, which
without icount is cpu_get_clock() under a seqlock. A new flag,
ARM_CP_IO_READ_NOBQL, makes reads of these registers skip the BQL and,
without icount, not end the TB. Writes and every other ARM_CP_IO
register are unchanged. Based on QEMU v11.1.2.

diff --git a/target/arm/cpregs.h b/target/arm/cpregs.h
index 391c0e3..3d5157e 100644
--- a/target/arm/cpregs.h
+++ b/target/arm/cpregs.h
@@ -154,6 +154,13 @@ enum {
      * ARM pseudocode function CheckFPMREnabled().
      */
     ARM_CP_FPMR                  = 1 << 23,
+    /*
+     * Flag: an ARM_CP_IO register whose readfn may run without the BQL
+     * (the generic timer counters: QEMU_CLOCK_VIRTUAL is read under a
+     * seqlock, the offsets are per-CPU state). Reads then skip the BQL and,
+     * without icount, do not end the TB. Writes are unaffected.
+     */
+    ARM_CP_IO_READ_NOBQL         = 1 << 24,
 };
 
 /*
